// Every check made to fail on purpose, once (M22 item 2).
//
//   node estate/breakall.js                 every mutant, two at a time, each check's clean run first
//   node estate/breakall.js nav fit         only mutants whose check's name contains these
//   node estate/breakall.js -j 1            one at a time
//   node estate/breakall.js --no-baseline   skip the clean runs (faster; a mutant "caught" by a check
//                                             that is red anyway is then NOT distinguishable - see below)
//   node estate/breakall.js --list          the mutants, and which checks have none
//
// WHY. A check that cannot fail proves nothing, and this project has shipped both kinds: a check whose filter
// excluded the very lines carrying two live bugs, and a check that ran for weeks and could never pass because its
// watch window was shorter than the thing it watched. Neither is visible from a green run. The only way to know a
// check can see the thing it names is to break that thing and watch it go red.
//
// HOW. A mutant is ONE TEXTUAL EDIT to one file - a page, a script, a data file - written below as the exact text
// it finds and the text it puts there. For each mutant this:
//   1. stages a throwaway copy of the repository (stage.js) - the real files are never touched;
//   2. applies the edit to the copy, and REFUSES if the text it looks for is not there EXACTLY ONCE: a mutant
//      whose target has moved is reported STALE, never silently skipped, because a mutant that edits nothing
//      "survives" for the wrong reason and one that edits nothing and is caught is a lie the other way;
//   3. serves the copy's site on a free port and runs the COPY of the check with RF_SITE pointing at it and every
//      fixed port moved by RF_PORT_OFFSET, so nothing here shares a port with anybody's suite;
//   4. says CAUGHT only if the check exited non-zero AND its output carries the line the mutant is about
//      (`expect`). A check that CRASHES on a mutant has not caught it - it has fallen over - and a check that
//      fails on some other line has not shown it can see THIS. Both are reported as NOT CAUGHT, with the output.
//
// THE CLEAN RUN, and why it is on by default. A mutant caught by a check that is red anyway proves nothing: the
// check would have exited 1 with or without it. So each check is first run once on an unmutated copy, and a
// mutant on a check whose clean run is red is reported as UNPROVEN, not as caught.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, said here because the runner exists to stop that being forgotten:
//   - ONE MUTANT PER BEHAVIOUR NAMED, NOT PER ASSERTION. Most checks assert ten or twenty things and a mutant
//     shows ONE of those lines can go red. The others are not shown by this file; where a check carries its own
//     in-memory mutants (mapsizecheck, holdasbuiltcheck, the proofs) or BREAK= modes, those cover more.
//   - A mutant is a guess at a likely break. A break of a different shape can still pass a check that catches
//     every mutant here.
//   - The weather: a check that skips (NOT RUN) on a mutant has not caught it, and is reported as such.
